<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ⚠ Exactly one directory, and it is the one wry's generated chrome client
  actually writes into. This file is androidx.core.content.FileProvider's
  allowlist: every path named here is a directory the provider will mint a
  content URI for and hand to another application.

  Tauri's template shipped an external-path entry over "." and a cache-path entry
  over ".". The first resolves to Environment.getExternalStorageDirectory() — the
  whole of shared external storage, so every file on the device's user visible
  volume was inside this provider's reach. The second is this app's cache root,
  and nothing in this tree has ever asked for a URI into it.

  What is real: RustWebChromeClient.kt creates a temporary JPEG under
  getExternalFilesDir(Environment.DIRECTORY_PICTURES) and passes it to
  FileProvider.getUriForFile, to give a camera app somewhere to write when a
  page's file input carries the capture attribute. That directory is
  external-files-path's Pictures/, and it is the only thing here that has ever
  needed a content URI. host_save_file is not a second consumer: it opens the
  platform's own save panel and writes with std::fs::write, so no URI is minted
  and no other application is involved.

  The provider is narrowed rather than removed because that chrome client is
  generated — `tauri android init` rewrites it — so the capture path can come back
  without anybody editing it, and a missing provider there degrades to a logged
  exception and the plain file picker rather than to anything a person sees. No
  input in packages/web carries capture today, which is why this is a narrowing
  and not a deletion. nativecheck compares the entries as an exact list.
-->
<paths xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <external-files-path name="captured_images" path="Pictures/" />
</paths>
